def ins(f, anchor, text):
    s=open(f).read()
    assert anchor in s, anchor[:40]
    s=s.replace(anchor, text+anchor,1)
    open(f,'w').write(s)

ins('s2.py','P("Combining Eqs. (@flux@) and (@energy@) gives',
 'P("The memory interpretation of Eq. (@equiv@) is useful for the discussion later. The Caputo derivative of order $1+\\\\alpha$ of a field at rest equals a Riemann–Liouville integral of order $1-\\\\alpha$ of the second time derivative, so that the fractional lag term is a weighted average of the past accelerations of the temperature with a power-law kernel $(t-s)^{-\\\\alpha}$. A weight that decays slowly gives the tissue a long memory of its heating history, and the parameter $\\\\tau_0^{\\\\alpha}$ sets the strength of this memory. For $\\\\alpha=1$ the kernel collapses to a delta function and the memory disappears, leaving the instantaneous second derivative. This is why $\\\\alpha=1$ is the integer-order hyperbolic limit and why smaller $\\\\alpha$ moves the model towards diffusion in the sense of a heavier tail of the response, although the model does not reduce to the Pennes equation for any $\\\\alpha>0$ when $\\\\tau_0>0$.")\n')
ins('s2.py','H2("2.4 Laser source")',
 'P("Two properties of the law should be recorded. The multiplier is non-negative by construction, so that the perfusion term $W_i\\\\theta_i$ has the sign of $\\\\theta_i^2$ and removes energy from the tissue at every point where the temperature differs from the blood temperature. The law therefore cannot create energy, and the energy balance of the model remains dissipative with respect to the perfusion mechanism. Second, the product $\\\\omega(\\\\theta)\\\\theta$ is not monotone: it increases for small $\\\\theta$, reaches a maximum near the critical temperature and falls to zero in the stasis region. The sink is therefore a non-monotone function of temperature, and the steady-state problem of Section 3.5 may in principle have more than one solution for large perfusion numbers. In the computations below a single branch was found, and the Picard iteration always started from the solution of the previous time level, which follows the branch continuously.")\n')
ins('s3.py','H2("3.2 Weak form and finite element discretisation")',
 'P("Two remarks on the reference solution complete this subsection. The inversion needs $s\\\\neq0$, which the Durbin contour guarantees, and the transformed solution has a branch point at $s=0$ for $\\\\alpha<1$ because of the non-integer power. The principal branch of the power is used and the contour lies to the right of the branch cut, so that no special treatment is required. The second remark concerns the two wave numbers. When the thermal and elastic wave numbers are close for some $s$, the eigenvectors could become ill-conditioned, but the weak coupling keeps them well separated, and the first-row and second-row alternatives of the eigenvector formula cover the remaining cases. No loss of precision of the $8\\\\times8$ solution was observed, and the agreement with the finite element solution to the level of four to five digits for $\\\\alpha=0.6$ in the smooth ramp test supports this.")\n')
ins('s3.py','P("The cost per time step is proportional',
 'P("Two properties of this time discretisation are relevant to the accuracy statements of Section 4. The convolution quadrature based on BDF2 has order two for data that are smooth and compatible with the zero initial state, which means that the field and its first time derivative vanish at $t=0$ in a sufficiently regular way. The step in the surface temperature violates this condition at the surface, and the observed first-order behaviour is the known consequence of a missing starting correction. Corrections that restore the second order exist, but they require the expansion of the solution near $t=0$ in powers of $t^{\\\\beta}$ with exponents that depend on the layered problem and on $\\\\alpha$, and they were not added. The second property is stability: the BDF2 generating function is A-stable, so that no restriction links the time step to the mesh size, which is the reason why steps much larger than the transit time of a front across one element can be used.")\n')
ins('s4.py','H2("4.6 Effect of the fractional order")',
 'P("A word on the size of the thermoelastic feedback is in order. The coupling coefficient $a_{51}=" + sc(a1["a5"], 2) + "$ is the ratio of the heat absorbed by the volumetric deformation to the heat stored thermally, and with its value the effect of the displacement on the temperature is below the plotting accuracy in all figures: switching the coupling off changes the temperature by a relative amount of the order of $a_5$ times the dilatation, which is about $10^{-4}$ here. The temperature field is therefore a thermal problem on its own, and the displacement and the stress are driven by it. This property simplifies the interpretation of the fractional and perfusion effects, since they act on the stress only through the temperature, but it also shows that the coupled character of the MGL theory is not tested by the dimensionless setup inherited from the source study. A dimensional setup with a length scale of millimetres would not change $a_5$ much, since it depends on the product $l\\\\nu$, which is the thermal diffusivity of the tumour, and not on $l$ alone, so the weakness of the feedback is a property of the material data of Table 1.")\n')
ins('s4.py','FIG("Figure_7.png"',
 'P("The reflection of the thermal wave at the isothermal far face, which occurs at about $t=0.32$ for the integer-order model, raises the temperature in the normal tissue again. For late times the temperature profile approaches the linear steady profile of a slab held at one and zero, and this is the reason for the weak dependence of the late-time temperature on the model at $t=0.6$ visible in Figures 4 and 5.")\n')
